akr 0.1
project lege-ecosystem

record lege-ecosystem.decision.a-fold-may-not-change-a-letters-counters/1 : decision {
    title "Truetyping never folds shapes that enclose different counters, and a stroke is far even when it is a hairline"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-process/encoding/bitrows.rs", path "lege-process/encoding/glyphfont.rs" ]
    decision """
        Two gates now guard the glyph dictionary against folding one letter into another. First, topology: a shape's enclosed counters are counted (`holes`, a flood fill from the border) and no match is considered across a different count — `c` never becomes `e`, `h` never becomes `b`, `a` never becomes `u`. The count is only trusted where the strokes have an interior, because a wall one pixel thick is opened or closed by a single speck. Second, the strict gate gained the far-pixel test the tolerant gate already had: ink that has none of the other shape's within a pixel of it, capped at 1% of the ink (minimum 2) with no far group larger than 2. A change of impression puts its difference along the strokes it thickens, so it is never far; a stroke one shape has and the other lacks is far along its length, however thin it is. Strict matches are also ranked by that structural agreement first, so rejecting a candidate never hands the instance to a worse one that survived.
        """
    context """
        A user's own run of the ITU T.88 specification came back with `which` as `wbicb`, `the` as `tbe`, `decoded` as `deeoded`, `are` as `ure` — whole letters replaced, in body text, in the format that had just been made the default. The fold dump showed why: the tolerant gate forgives ink within a pixel of the other shape, and at reading sizes the crossbar of an `e` is four pixels long with both ends touching the bowl, so only two pixels are left to notice. `far_blob <= 3` was meant to catch a missing stroke and cannot, because a stroke at that size is not three pixels of anything. The strict gate had no far test at all: its 2x2-block measure sees nothing in a one-pixel hairline, so it merged `a` with `e` and `u` with `n` on a heavier face.
        """
    consequences """
        Dictionaries grow: 3 pages of Gobineau 150 to 160 shapes, 5 pages of T.88 372 to 414, 5 pages of the Gernet scan 443 to 465 — 3-10%, and the fonts with them. Splitting is the safe direction: two prototypes for one letter cost bytes, one prototype for two letters costs the reader the word. Counters are computed per component (a flood fill alongside the ink profile) and prune candidates, so the tolerant stage got faster rather than slower. A scan whose counter is filled in by heavy ink now keeps its own prototype instead of folding; that is a split, not a substitution. Text on a source that was already binarized at low resolution still merges touching pairs (`in` reads as `m`), which no pixel budget separates at ten pixels a letter.
        """
    claim a-hairline-stroke-is-far-not-thick {
        text """
            The 2x2-block `thick` measure cannot see a one-pixel stroke, which is why the strict gate merged `a` with `e`; the far-pixel measure sees it as a connected run along the stroke's length and is now bounded for both gates.
            """
    }
    claim counters-are-identity {
        text """
            A letter's enclosed counters survive every change of weight, so a match across a different count is always wrong; `find_match` refuses it whenever both shapes have stroke interiors.
            """
    }
    claim rejection-alone-does-not-make-matching-safer {
        text """
            The search keeps the best candidate it is offered, so narrowing a filter that can exclude the *right* prototype (the dimension slack, or a far bound tight enough to reject a broken edge) hands the instance to the next best wrong one instead. Filters that are semantic (a different letter) help; filters that are about quality (a damaged instance) must rank rather than reject.
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.jbig2-is-the-default-pdf-text-format-again/1 : decision {
    title "JBIG2 is the Freya GUI's default PDF text encoder again; truetyping is the opt-in checkbox"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-process/GUI/Freya/**", path "lege-process/language_service/**" ]
    decision """
        The Freya GUI's default PDF text encoder is JBIG2 symbol mode again. Truetyping stays a first-class format, keeps its name everywhere, and is offered as a checkbox (TrueTyping text) that excludes compatibility mode; clearing either checkbox returns to JBIG2. CompressionType::default() is Jbig2 and ProcessingOptions::new() starts there. The CLI is unchanged: its non-interactive default was and is ccitt4, truetyping is --truetyping or format 4.
        """
    context """
        On the 886-page archive.org scan crusadeswholesto0000lamb_1.pdf (RGB JPEG pages), after the glyph dictionary was made parallel, the three text encoders on the same debug-fast build and machine came out at: JBIG2 33 s and 21.2 MB, CCITT4 35 s and 27.7 MB, truetyping 120 s and 45.3 MB. Truetyping is 3.6x slower and 2.1x larger than JBIG2 on a real long book, and 41 MB of its output is 22 embedded fonts: position variants, compounds and text variants use about 1000 glyph ids per page, so a 65533-id font fills every 40-odd pages and each new font re-learns the alphabet. The 20-page comparison that justified the earlier default (183 KB against 378 KB) did not see this. The user asked for the reversal on 2026-09-05 once the numbers were confirmed.
        """
    consequences """
        Users who saved settings while truetyping was the default keep an explicit Truetyping in their saved options and stay on it until they clear the checkbox; nothing migrates them. The JBIG2 no-symbol toggle is visible again on the default path. Truetyping's remaining cost to bring down before it can be a default is glyph-id consumption per page, not matching speed.
        """
    supersedes [ @lege-ecosystem.decision.truetyping-is-the-default-pdf-text-format ]
    supported_by [
        @lege-ecosystem.evidence.truetyping-parallel-probe-benchmark-2026-09-05,
        @lege-ecosystem.observation.truetyping-dictionary-lock-serialized-every-page
    ]
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.jbig2-symbol-substitution-is-opt-in/1 : decision {
    title "JBIG2 writes the bit-exact generic region unless symbol mode is asked for"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-process/**" ]
    topic jbig2-symbol-default
    decision """
        The JBIG2 text encoder defaults to the bit-exact generic region. Symbol substitution is opt-in, and when it is asked for it is the unifying mode (Jbig2Mode::SymUnify), never the plain symbol dictionary: a plain dictionary already risks a wrong match and unification is what pays for that risk in bytes. The GUI carries a 'Symbol mode' checkbox (off by default) that sends --sym-unify; --symbol-mode and --jbig2-mode symbol still reach the middle mode from the CLI, and --no-symbol keeps working as an explicit no-op.
        """
    context """
        Symbol substitution was the default from 2026-08-29 with --no-symbol as the opt-out. Substitution errors are silent and change words, and the user saw one in truetyping output on a bolded line ('aoil' for 'soil'). A default that can rewrite text is the wrong default for a reader-facing converter. On 41 pages of a 240-dpi book scan the generic region cost 895 KB against 878 KB for --sym-unify (1.9%), so the safe default is nearly free on noisy scans.
        """
    consequences """
        Default JBIG2 output grows by a few percent on scans and more on clean text, where symbol matching pays best. PipelineConfig, Jbig2Settings and the interactive menu all default to Generic, so a library caller that never sets a mode now gets bit-exact output. set_jbig2_symbol_mode(true) selects SymUnify.
        """
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.jp2-region-floor-splits-by-region-size/1 : decision {
    title "The verified JP2 display floor for an ordinary figure region is 65 when it covers under a quarter of the page and 70 otherwise"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "lege-process/pipeline/helper_functions.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/quality_policy.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        quality_policy::region_jp2_floor takes a RegionSize: an ordinary (non-HQ, non-cover) region whose pixel area is under one quarter of the output page's area gets floor 65, a larger one keeps 70; HQ (78) and cover (75) are unchanged, as are the full-page base floor (55), the grayscale region floor (65) and the legacy open-loop presets used only as fallback.
        """
    context """
        After the jp2lam perceptual work the floor is a guaranteed display-size score rather than an open-loop preset, so the same number buys the same visible fidelity whatever the region's pixel count. The user asked on 2026-09-06 to bring the quality down a little more, about 65 for small images and 70 for big ones.
        """
    consequences """
        Small figures cost fewer bytes; large plates and covers look as before. Size is measured on the region's placement box against the output page (RegionSize::of), threaded through region_encoding_settings and encode_region_image. Not yet measured on a corpus; the sweep in quality_policy.rs's comment covered the 70 floor only.
        """
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.jp2lam-ssim-canonical-metric/1 : decision {
    title "jp2lam encode-time quality is shared JPXL SSIMULACRA2"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-codecs/jp2lam/**" ]
    topic jp2lam-ssim-controller
    decision """
        jp2lam's encode-time perceptual quality target is SSIMULACRA2, using the same production implementation and metric version as JPXL (`ssimulacra2-jpxl-1` in jpegXL-rs/JPXL/crates/jpxl-perceptual). A requested score of 90.0 means the same floor in both codecs. The rust-av ssimulacra2 crate remains a test-only parity oracle. Butteraugli remains a measurement column (examples/perceptual_curve.rs and corpus screens), not the encode-time objective and not a veto. Do not introduce a second production SSIMULACRA2.
        """
    context """
        The 2026-09-04 ssimulacrascheduler plan and the existing JPXL perceptual-quality contract. jp2lam already has perceptually weighted PCRD (local allocator) and a rust-av scoring harness. Sharing the JPXL in-tree metric is what makes a later score-matched byte comparison valid. Mixing rust-av scores with in-tree scores in one table is a known JPXL failure (jpegxl-rs.observation.pqc-pr4-rate-curve-axis-audit-2026-08-23).
        """
    consequences """
        jp2lam depends on jpxl-perceptual (path, default-features=false). RateControl::Perceptual will take a SSIMULACRA2 score and effort, not a Butteraugli distance. Quality(u8) stays the legacy baseline until an explicit mapping session. lege-ecosystem.encode-quality.improve-photographic-rate-control's remaining 'butteraugli-anchored quality mapping' is no longer the encode-time objective.
        """
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.jp2lam-verified-quality-scale/1 : decision {
    title "jp2lam quality number is the verified SSIMULACRA2 scale; legacy ladder is ApproxQuality"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-codecs/jp2lam/**" ]
    topic jp2lam-quality-scale
    decision """
        The user-facing jp2lam quality scale is the verified SSIMULACRA2 scale: `RateControl::Quality { level, effort }` means a measured `ssimulacra2-jpxl-1` floor of `level` (0..=99, 100 = Lossless), emitting the smallest exact stream that meets it. The legacy open-loop rate ladder is preserved unchanged under `RateControl::ApproxQuality(u8)` (and the `EncodeOptions.quality` field when `rate_control` is absent) and is documented as an approximate, unmeasured preset. It is not recalibrated to the corpus bytes-per-pixel prior. `RateControl::Perceptual(PerceptualTarget)` remains the expert raw-score form. The corpus bpp-vs-score calibration is a navigation prior for the controller's first probe, never a quality definition. No legacy API compatibility is preserved (jp2lam is 0.3.x); `EncodeOptions::photo`, the photo presets and the CLI `qN` form are on the verified scale, and `--approx qN` / the auto content classifier select the legacy preset.
        """
    context """
        User decision 2026-09-05 after the Session 8c calibration: legacy preset 80 measured SSIMULACRA2 52 at 0.8 MP, 30 at 4-6 MP and 14 at 50 MP (perceptual score 80 needs ~1.5 bpp; preset 80 spends 0.77 bpp at 0.8 MP), so an open-loop rate mapping cannot truthfully implement a unified perceptual scale and repointing it would change every consumer's sizes without the verified semantics. Supersedes the 'Quality(u8) stays the legacy baseline until an explicit mapping session' consequence of lege-ecosystem.decision.jp2lam-ssim-canonical-metric: this is that session.
        """
    consequences """
        q80 means SSIMULACRA2 >= 80 in both JPXL and jp2lam; sizes differ by codec, meaning does not. Verified quality requires 8-bit gray/sRGB (documents, ICC and CMYK stay on ApproxQuality / explicit rate targets). The per-image feature predictor is added only if the static bpp prior cannot keep Fast in the 1-3 probe range.
        """
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.nup-grid-is-read-in-the-sheets-own-frame/1 : decision {
    title "The N-up cell grid is read in the sheet's own frame, not transposed for a landscape sheet"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-pdf/print/src/layout.rs" ]
    decision """
        NUp::grid() is applied to whichever way the sheet is turned, rather than being treated as portrait-relative and transposed when the sheet is landscape. A booklet is the exception: it is always two cells side by side, because the fold is always the vertical centre line.
        """
    context """
        Transposing keeps each cell's shape fixed relative to the paper, which is the intuitive reading, but it costs paper on the two non-square grids. Measured on A4 pages onto A4 paper under FitToPage and Orientation::Auto: two-up falls from 0.707 to 0.500 and six-up from 0.354 to 0.333. The square grids -- four-, nine- and sixteen-up -- are unaffected either way.
        """
    consequences """
        Reading the grid in the sheet's frame is also exactly the conventional CUPS arrangement: a landscape sheet with two columns for two-up, three-by-two for six-up. Orientation::Auto finds those arrangements unaided, because they score strictly better on the visible-fraction-then-coverage metric it already uses.
        """
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.office-printing ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.printing-defers-prepress-to-lege-pdf-write/1 : decision {
    title "Office printing is built; prepress is deferred, and belongs in lege-pdf/write rather than the rasterizer"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-pdf/print/**", path "lege-pdf/render/**", path "lege-pdf/write/**" ]
    decision """
        lege-pdf-print implements office printing only -- paper, imposition, sheet composition, spooling -- and explicitly defers prepress: CMYK separations, an output ICC profile and rendering intent, overprint simulation, halftone screening and PDF/X conformance. When prepress is wanted, it is to be built in lege-pdf/write emitting PDF/X, not by teaching the display rasterizer to produce CMYK.
        """
    context """
        The renderer's frozen surface contract has exactly two OutputFormat variants, Rgba8PremultipliedSrgb and Gray8; colour conversion is one-directional into sRGB by policy; and overprint is detected into a PageFeatures flag and explicitly not simulated. Every prepress want is therefore blocked on a contract-level change.
        """
    consequences """
        A commercial printer wants a file, not a bitmap, and that file is a PDF/X -- which lege-pdf/write already has the OutputIntent and font-embedding machinery for, and which phase 0's Trim and Bleed parsing now supplies boxes to. The renderer would be involved only for on-screen soft-proofing, which is the last piece to build rather than the first.
        """
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.office-printing ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.sideways-pages-are-turned-upright-with-rotate/1 : decision {
    title "A sideways page is turned upright with /Rotate, never on its head"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "lege-pdf/write/src/artifact.rs",
        path "lege-pdf/write/src/pages.rs",
        path "lege-process/core/pdf_artifact.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/pdf_tokio_pipeline.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        The PDF writer emits `/Rotate` on the page dictionary, and Lege sets it from the quarter turns the orientation detector already measures, so a page scanned sideways opens upright. Only the sideways turns (90 and 270) are acted on; a 180 reading is ignored. Nothing is resampled and no coordinate changes: the raster, the OCR text layer and the glyph text keep the space they were written in and turn together.
        """
    context """
        Orientation detection has shipped since pass 6 and drives OCR (a sideways CJK scan went from 27 to about 1100 words a page), but the output was never turned: `straighten.rs` puts every glyph back where it was scanned. The user made the GUI default conditional on sideways pages being flippable in production, so the flip had to become real rather than possible. The detector calls a page sideways only when the turned page's baselines are half again as sharp as the page's own, but calls it upside down on a margin of 1.15, which is a much weaker signal.
        """
    consequences """
        A page whose orientation nothing measured is left alone: the frame is computed for every page under truetyping, but only when OCR is on in the other text formats. `/Rotate` is written per page, never inherited from `/Pages`, so a book may mix orientations. DjVu has no equivalent attribute and would need the raster itself turned, so DjVu output is unchanged. The requested height was applied to the sideways render, so an upright page comes out taller than the device asked for.
        """
    claim no-180-turn {
        text """
            Turns of 2 are dropped rather than written: the upside-down margin is 1.15 against the sideways 1.5, and a page wrongly stood on its head is worse for an e-ink reader than one left as scanned.
            """
    }
    claim rotate-from-quarter-turns {
        text """
            `PageFrame.turns` counts clockwise quarter turns that make the text upright, which is exactly the `/Rotate` convention, so the value passes through unchanged (`core/pdf_artifact.rs::display_rotation`).
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.simple-cli-default-binarization-is-adaptive/1 : decision {
    title "The simple CLI mode binarizes adaptively by default, not with a fixed 180 threshold"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-process/core/main.rs", path "lege-process/pipeline/config.rs" ]
    topic cli-default-binarization
    decision """
        PipelineConfig::simple_cli_defaults sets use_fixed_threshold=false so a bare `lege book.pdf` runs the Sauvola + background-normalised Otsu binarizer that the help text has always documented as the default.
        """
    context """
        Since the initial pipeline commit the simple-mode default was a fixed threshold of 180 while --help said adaptive. Yellowed scans (crusades, globalhistory, Gernet) render their paper near gray 150, so every page came out solid black in any bilevel text format and TrueTyping saw one page-sized component and zero glyphs. The GUI, --image-debug and the music-sheet path already forced adaptive, which is why it went unnoticed. Found on 2026-09-05 while validating the review fixes.
        """
    consequences """
        Simple-mode output on bright-paper scans changes slightly (adaptive instead of a 180 cut). --binarization threshold and --threshold still select the fixed cut explicitly. The mrc-adaptive-mask special case in main.rs that forced adaptive is now redundant but harmless.
        """
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.top-level-application-license-agpl/1 : decision {
    title "License the top-level Lege applications under AGPL-3.0-only"
    state active
    scope [
        path "Cargo.toml",
        path "LICENSE",
        path "NOTICE",
        path "README.md",
        path "lege-android/**",
        path "lege-misc/docs/licenses.html",
        path "lege-process/**",
        path "lege-viewer/**"
    ]
    topic top-level-application-license
    decision """
        The Lege top-level workspace and combined application products are licensed AGPL-3.0-only, authorizing direct linking to the AGPL-3.0-only DJVULibRust crate. The root and application package metadata and included license texts must say AGPL-3.0-only. Independently usable codec and third-party subtrees retain their explicit licenses: DJVULibRust remains AGPL-3.0-only, while jbig2enc-rust and jp2lam remain MIT OR Apache-2.0.
        """
    context """
        The user selected the plan's recommended D1 outcome: everything is AGPL again at the top level. The former MIT application declaration existed to support the now-retired arms-length subprocess boundary.
        """
    consequences """
        Lege may restore the in-process DJVULibRust API. Application crates that directly link lege-process, including lege-android, must not continue to claim MIT. Mixed-license subtrees and third-party notices remain explicitly mapped rather than being relabeled.
        """
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.open-source-monorepo-relaunch/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @lege-ecosystem.evidence.djvu-license-linkage-boundary-audit-20260822/1,
        @lege-ecosystem.evidence.djvu-subprocess-license-surface-audit-20260822/1
    ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.truetyping-banks-fonts-and-respects-a-bilevel-source/1 : decision {
    title "A document may carry several glyph fonts, and a book already black and white is traced at its own resolution"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "lege-pdf/read/src/session.rs",
        path "lege-pdf/write/src/**",
        path "lege-process/encoding/glyphfont.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/config.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/pdf_tokio_pipeline.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        Two changes take the ceiling off long books. A TrueType font holds 65536 glyph ids, so the session now keeps a dictionary per *bank*: a page whose components would carry the current dictionary past that limit starts the next one, every page draws from exactly one font, and the writer emits `/F2`, `/F3`, … with a reserved id per bank. And a page whose ink is already one bit — a full-page stencil, or a full-page image with a one-bit soft mask — has no more detail to give at any scale, so truetyping is rendered at that resolution rather than at its own working height. A greyscale or colour scan keeps the raised height: its soft edges resolve *better* the finer they are sampled.
        """
    context """
        An 836-page book failed after about seven minutes with `PDF writer actor has stopped`. It was the 16-bit glyph id space: the page adapter rejects an id past 65535 and the writer actor died on it, while the real message was lost because the sender only sees a closed channel. The same run was slow for a second reason — the book was itself a Lege output, a greyscale JP2 under a JBIG2 stencil at 1200 px, and rendering it at 2400 invented interpolated edges that no two impressions of a letter shared: 120 pages made 24 381 glyphs in 3 minutes at 2400 px and 9 363 in 18 seconds at 1400.
        """
    consequences """
        The book now finishes in 3 minutes with two fonts. The writer actor records what it died of before dropping its receiver, so a page worker reports the cause rather than the symptom. Bank assignment is decided before a page is analysed, from the component count plus headroom, so a page never straddles two fonts and no composite ever crosses one. `GlyphFontSession::with_prototype_cap` exists so the split can be tested without 65 000 shapes.
        """
    claim bilevel-sources-have-a-ceiling {
        text """
            `CompiledDocumentPage::bilevel_raster_height` reports the full-page-equivalent height of a page's one-bit ink layer (the stencil itself, or a one-bit soft mask) and nothing for a continuous-tone page, and the pipeline lowers the truetyping working height to it, never below the height the caller asked for.
            """
    }
    claim one-font-per-page {
        text """
            `PreparedGlyphLayer::font` names the bank a page's glyph ids index; the writer reserves one Type0 id per bank and writes each font at finalize, so a page never draws from two.
            """
    }
    claim the-actor-says-what-it-died-of {
        text """
            The writer actor stores its error in a `OnceLock` the handle shares before it drops the receiver, so a send that finds the channel closed reports `PDF writer failed: <cause>` instead of `PDF writer actor has stopped`.
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.truetyping-is-the-default-pdf-text-format/1 : decision {
    title "Truetyping is the name and the default for PDF text output"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "lege-process/GUI/Freya/**",
        path "lege-process/color/color_processing.rs",
        path "lege-process/core/main.rs",
        path "lege-process/language_service/**",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/config.rs",
        path "lege-process/pipeline/pdf_tokio_pipeline.rs"
    ]
    decision """
        The glyph-font text format is called `truetyping` in every user-facing surface and is the default PDF text encoder in the Freya GUI. `glyphfont` stays an accepted spelling of the CLI and config value forever, normalized at parse time, so scripted runs and saved settings keep working; the Rust module, the log prefixes and the AKR history keep the name they were built under, because they name the mechanism rather than the product. In the GUI the two text-encoder opt-outs are checkboxes that exclude each other by construction: `CompressionType` gained a `Truetyping` variant and is now the single field the text encoder is read from, so JBIG2 and compatibility mode (CCITT4 + baseline JPEG) cannot both be on, and clearing either returns to truetyping. Detected image regions are a separate three-way choice: Original, Dithered, or JBIG2 halftone. Halftone remains available with either truetyping or JBIG2 text and is embedded as its own JBIG2 overlay; compatibility mode and DjVu do not offer it.
        """
    context """
        Seven passes brought the format to parity: on a 20-page scan it is 183 KB against JBIG2 symbol mode's 378 KB at equal or better legibility, and the user signed off on the rendered outputs. The old `effective_text_format` derived the encoder from `image_processing_type` whenever layout analysis was on, so the GUI's real default was CCITT4 and the compatibility checkbox's `compression_type` flip was a no-op on the default path. Making the text encoder an explicit field removes that coupling: how image regions are treated no longer decides how text is written.
        """
    consequences """
        Text encoding and detected-image encoding remain orthogonal. Choosing Dithered or JBIG2 halftone does not switch the text encoder; the halftone choice stays visible with truetyping and JBIG2 text, while compatibility mode keeps its CCITT4 + baseline-JPEG promise and DjVu keeps its native image path. Raster reflow re-renders every page as an image and has no text encoder, so it refuses truetyping on the CLI and is not offered it in the GUI. The Freya GUI is the only frontend changed: the sheet-music GUI and lege-viewer carry their own copies of the option model and keep the old default.
        """
    claim halftone-images-are-independent {
        text """
            The Freya image row presents Original, Dithered, and JBIG2 halftone as exclusive choices. The durable `image_processing_type` plus `use_jbig2_halftone` fields preserve old saved settings; the worker forwards `--halftone` for both truetyping and JBIG2 text, and the PDF pipeline emits the detected image crop as a JBIG2 halftone overlay with its own globals.
            """
    }
    claim image-handling-no-longer-picks-the-encoder {
        text """
            `effective_text_format` no longer consults `image_processing_type` or `layout_analysis`; before this change Original meant CCITT4 and Dithered meant JBIG2 whenever layout analysis was on, which made CCITT4 the GUI's actual default.
            """
    }
    claim one-field-decides-the-encoder {
        text """
            The Freya GUI reads the PDF text encoder from `CompressionType` alone (`Truetyping | Jbig2 | Ccitt4`); `ProcessingOptions::set_text_encoder` keeps `jpeg_compat` and the text-encoder-specific sub-options in step, which is what makes the two checkboxes mutually exclusive.
            """
    }
    claim truetyping-is-canonical {
        text """
            `truetyping` is the canonical text_format value; `glyphfont` is normalized to it in `PipelineConfig::set_text_format` and in the CLI's `--text-format` parser, so the pipeline compares one constant (`pipeline::config::TRUETYPING`).
            """
    }
    derived_from [ @lege-ecosystem.work.glyph-font-text-output ]
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.truetyping-leaves-bilevel-sources-alone/1 : decision {
    title "Truetyping is skipped when the source's ink is already one bit; source masks pass through"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-process/encoding/glyphfont.rs", path "lege-process/pipeline/**" ]
    decision """
        A source page whose ink already comes from a one-bit image or a one-bit soft mask (a JBIG2 or CCITT text layer, an MRC mask) is original text: truetyping does not trace it. If the sampled pages of a document are mostly bilevel (bilevel_source_height), the run switches its text format to jbig2 before any page is processed and gives back the height the caller asked for; a single bilevel page in a scanned book drops its glyph session and is encoded as the jbig2 format would (page_ink_is_bilevel); and a preserved JBIG2 SMask passes through untouched under truetyping exactly as it does under jbig2 and ccitt4.
        """
    context """
        The preserved-mask path already passed the source mask through for jbig2 and ccitt4, but truetyping replaced it with glyph text traced from the decoded mask, and a plain bilevel book was traced at its own resolution. Tracing one-bit ink can only move its edges, and the font it makes is a lossy copy of something the input already held losslessly.
        """
    consequences """
        Truetyping on a lege JBIG2 output is a JBIG2 re-encode at the requested height (20 pages: 0.56 MB in 8 s). clamp_truetyping_render_height is gone; set_text_format restores the requested height when leaving truetyping. Implemented 2026-09-06 in lege-process/pipeline/pdf_tokio_pipeline.rs and config.rs.
        """
    depends_on [ @lege-ecosystem.decision.jbig2-is-the-default-pdf-text-format-again ]
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}

record lege-ecosystem.decision.truetyping-renders-in-grayscale-by-default/1 : decision {
    title "Truetyping renders its ink mask through the MRC clean path"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "lege-process/**" ]
    topic truetyping-render-mode
    decision """
        Choosing truetyping selects grayscale/MRC rendering. In the CLI, --text-format truetyping implies --grayscale unless a bilevel mode is named (--binarization, --threshold, --sauvola-k); in the GUI, ticking TrueTyping flips the Binarized/Grayscale pair to Grayscale, which the user can flip back.
        """
    context """
        Truetyping draws the text as outlines over a background, so the ink mask only decides where the outlines go. Sauvola on a raw scan keeps the paper grain and every speck becomes a glyph; the MRC clean path despeckles it. On 41 pages of the crusades scan: 0.77 MB and 1347 shapes grayscale against 2.56 MB and 13087 bilevel, and the grayscale run was faster. Tested on a Kobo Clara BW: both load and page-turn acceptably, grayscale is considerably smaller.
        """
    consequences """
        Bilevel truetyping stays reachable by naming a binarization mode or unticking Grayscale. Truetyping remains opt-in overall (see @lege-ecosystem.decision.jbig2-is-the-default-pdf-text-format-again): dictionary matching still makes occasional substitution errors on bold text.
        """
    author "GitHub Uploader"
}
